  "scientific_conclusions": {
    "empirical_baseline_generalization": "On the independent 2025 season (24 races), the Empirical Grid Baseline achieves 0.5423 Brier Score and 66.7% Top-1 accuracy (16/24 correct winner picks), decisively outperforming Frozen Model A (0.6325 Brier, 54.2% Top-1) and Remediated Model B (0.6755 Brier, 54.2% Top-1). Model B Retrained on 2021-2024 marginally edges the empirical baseline on log loss (1.0680 vs 1.1250, p=0.48), but severely loses on probability sharpness and Brier score (+0.082 worse) and Top-1 accuracy (-12.5% worse).",
    "top1_winner_accuracy_dominance": "In 2025, 16 races were won from pole (P1). The Empirical Grid Baseline correctly identified all 16 pole winners (100%), whereas LightGBM ML models only predicted 13 winners, being distracted by secondary features (e.g. historical form, practice pace) when the pole-sitter converted cleanly.",
    "recommended_architecture": "Neither a pure ML model nor a pure empirical grid baseline is globally optimal alone. The empirical baseline provides vastly superior calibration and Brier reliability, while the ML model provides marginal value in separating pole vs P2/P3 contenders when grid delta is narrow. The recommended production prediction architecture for future phases is an ENSEMBLE/HYBRID model anchored to empirical grid prior probabilities with conservative shrinkage on ML session features."
  }
